<h2>The allegation and the defence</h2>
<p>The FIR, arising out of Birpur (Balua Bazar) P.S. Case No. 258 of 2023 in Supaul, alleged that eighteen named accused and others entered the informant's house. The petitioner was said to have fired at the informant — the shot missing and striking a pillar — and to have caused a lip injury to a woman with a blunt object. His case was straightforward: no criminal antecedents, false implication in a mass FIR, the only injury attributed to him simple in nature, and co-accused already granted anticipatory bail by a coordinate bench in an earlier application. The State opposed, but the court found it appropriate to extend pre-arrest protection.</p>

<h2>Why these applications succeed or fail</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Role specificity.</strong> Mass FIRs sweep in whole households. Courts separate those with a distinct overt act from those named in the crowd — and test even the distinct act against its documented consequence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Injury character.</strong> Simple injuries weigh toward bail; grievous injuries on vital parts, or injuries matching a dangerous weapon, weigh against. The medical report usually matters more than the FIR narrative.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity.</strong> A coordinate bench order protecting similarly placed co-accused is the single strongest card, and should be placed on record with the order copy and a role-comparison.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Antecedents.</strong> A clean record supports the discretionary grant; a history of similar offences invites refusal regardless of parity.</div>
</div>

<h2>The order\'s mechanics</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1 — Surrender window.</strong> The petitioner must appear before the court below within four weeks of the order.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2 — Bond.</strong> On arrest or surrender, release on a bond of ₹10,000 with two sureties of like amount, to the satisfaction of the jurisdictional ACJM.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3 — Statutory conditions.</strong> The grant operates subject to Section 482(2) BNSS — cooperation with the investigation, no tampering, and the other default conditions the provision imports.</div>
</div>

<h2>Context for Delhi practice</h2>
<p>Though decided in Patna, the structure of the order mirrors anticipatory bail practice everywhere, including the Delhi district courts and the Delhi High Court: the discretionary assessment runs on role, injury, antecedents and parity, while the operative portion fixes a surrender timeline and bond. Two practical points travel well. First, in multi-accused FIRs, obtain and annex every earlier protective order in the same case — parity cannot be applied to orders the court is not shown. Second, where the attributed injury is simple, say so with the injury report itself, not merely as an assertion; the difference between a simple and grievous classification is frequently the difference between protection and custody.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> A grant of anticipatory bail is not an acquittal and does not restrain the investigation. The petitioner who treats the surrender window casually, or skips interrogation dates, hands the prosecution its best argument for cancellation.</p>
</div>
